foreign  and welcome to today's webinar emerging  solutions for non-invasive cancer  monitoring I'm antonina Salcido of lab  roots and I'll be your moderator for  today's event  today's educational web seminar is  presented by labroots and brought to you  by new probe to learn more please visit  newprobe.com  we encourage you to participate today by  submitting any questions you may have  during the presentation to do so simply  type them into the ask a question box  and click Send  will answer as many questions as we have  time for at the end of the presentation  you may also submit any technical issues  here as well if you have trouble seeing  or hearing the presentation  I'd like to now welcome our speaker Dr  Nick gallerani scientific Affairs at new  Pro  Nick you may now begin your presentation  great thank you  good morning everyone and thank you for  joining today's session uh and thank you  to lab Roots also for organizing this  event my name is Nick elurani and I work  in scientific Affairs at new probe today  what I'm going to be talking about is a  new probe and a technology that we've  developed called blocker displacement  amplification or BDA which we have begun  to apply to non-invasive cancer  monitoring research  so there are three main objectives for  today's talk uh one is to briefly go  over the current state advantages and  limitations of non-invasive liquid  biopsies in oncology applications  the second goal is to introduce the BDA  technology and highlight some of those  applications that we've begun in  non-invasive longitudinal monitoring  and then lastly I want to go over the  new probe ourselves and highlight some  of the ways that these Technologies can  be accessed by working with new  Okay so  as you may be aware the emergence of  liquid biopsy has enabled many  non-invasive testing options for cancer  patients including recurrence monitoring  non-invasive tumor profiling and  non-invasive therapy selection as well  as early cancer screening  liquid biopsy can refer to a range of  different types of tests but for new  probe we're mainly developing Next  Generation sequencing based tests for  cell-free DNA analysis as well as  peripheral blood analysis in the case of  heme cancers such as acute myeloid  leukemia  and with test modalities like NGS we can  extract broad genetic information which  may give the patient insights into  things like disease prognosis which  drugs are likely to be effective and  which ones we might expect to see  resistance too  now one of the central challenges in  liquid biopsy is that the tumor  associate analytes themselves are quite  rare and in context such as monitoring  or early detection and cell-free DNA  that originates from the cancer may only  constitute 0.01 or even less of the  total fraction of cell-free DNA  and just to broaden the scope of this  problem a little bit for both solid  tumor and heme malignancy monitoring  applications we see similar Rarity  problems  in mrd we want to be able to detect  signs of recurrence earlier so that we  can also ACT earlier on the cancer  but if a patient is responding very well  the amounts of cancer Associated  analytes are going to decline  significantly to the point where they  may be undetectable by most methods even  if they have Trace remnants of disease  or small drug resistance clones which  may eventually Drive relapse  while there are certain error correcting  NGS methods that have been employed that  are able to detect mutations at very low  levels these may be costly to implement  and they also May potentially show poor  performance at single low side  so beyond just low concentration of  analytes and cost sensitivity balance a  lot of different challenges exist in the  liquid biopsy space before liquid  biopsy's potential can really be fully  realized  I think some of these can be considered  biological in nature some of these  include concordance between tissue and  liquid samples as well as detection of  other important genetic biomarkers such  as copy number variation  but there are also a lot of challenges  on the clinical implementation side some  of these include achieving that balance  of sensitivity and costs that I talked  about  standardizing pre-analytical variables  and then also what to do with all of the  results especially for some of these  ultra low frequency events  at the current stage that new probe is  at we're not we're not really addressing  all of these issues but  um you know as a company that is  interested in this space I think it's  important for everyone in this space to  understand the bigger picture of what  we're looking at  so like I said new probe is a is a  fairly small company that can only focus  on so many things so today what we  really want to focus on is the issue of  low concentration of analytes and  achieving this cost sensitivity balance  uh and so I'll introduce this technology  which is based on our BDA technology  called quantitative blocker displacement  amplification this was first featured in  a pub and 2021 publication in nature  Communications and this is available  through Open Access so nature  Communications website  Okay so  this technology blocker displacement  amplification is a rare allele  enrichment method which enables us to  selectively enrich mutant alleles while  suppressing amplification of wild-type  alleles during PCR  this enables us to sensitively detect  mutations at single loci below 0.01 vaf  and this method is flexible and can be  incorporated into a wide range of  modalities including qpcr and NGS From  newport's perspective but we've also  recently engaged in development of  digital PCR assays we recently announced  a deal with bio rad to incorporate BDA  technology into digital PCR assays so on  the horizon we can look out for new  highly sensitive digital PCR assays from  biorad that contain the same technology  so the way that the way that this  technology works is through a  competitive hybridization reaction  between a non-extensible oligonucleotide  called a blocker and an extensible  primer  the blocker is designed to be  complementary to a known DNA sequence in  this case the wild type template  well the primer is designed to have a  region of overlap between the between  the um  the blocker and primer  and so  so basically this this region of overlap  creates creates competition between  primer and blocker when a variant is  present within a 12 to 30 nucleotide  region uh which we call the  amplification window at the three prime  end of the blocker it creates a mismatch  this mismatch shifts the favorability of  primer binding such that the primer now  out competes the blocker for access to  the binding site at that region of  overlap  and over multiple Cycles at PCR we can  achieve significant enrichment of  variance while suppressing amplification  of the known sequence  and so over many rounds of PCR this can  lead to over a thousand-fold  magnification of those those variants  now several of these methods for rare  allele enrichment have been described in  literature but I think what  differentiates the BDA amplification  approach is really its scalability so  BDA enrichment can efficiently occur  over a broader range of temperatures  compared to some of these other methods  that have been described such as in this  case LNA clamp PCR  and this has enabled us to really scale  up BDA assays to NGS approaches allowing  low depth sequencing analysis of low vaf  mutations  now in order to effectively scale up to  these NGS  NGS levels we have also Incorporated  we've made a modification of the core  BDA technology and Incorporated unique  molecular identifiers into our BDA  approach and this is really what  quantitative blocker displacement  amplification is it's bdas plus umis to  improve quantitation and correct for  polymerase error that occurs during PCR  so experimentally uh in order to  demonstrate this benefit of reducing uh  the vile type read fraction  what we did first was we created a set  of reference materials which consisted  of wild type DNA that was mixed with  nine different synthetic DNA G blocks  each containing a different SNB or indel  at roughly one percent vaf  using a standard NGS approach without  enrichment 88 percent of the NGS reads  were wild type whereas with qvda  only about two percent of the total  reads were wild type we did observe a  small fraction of unexpected variance  likely introduced by polymerase errors  but because we have this Umi filtering  um and bioinformatics pipeline we're  able to effectively filter these false  variants out  so we then constructed a 20 Gene NGS  panel capable of detecting over 450 smvs  and indels in hot spots related to acute  wineline leukemia  and again using synthetic reference  material we compared qbda to NGS with  umis but without this enrichment and  even at vif levels below 0.01 percent  and in some cases well below 0.1 percent  we found that all the mutations were  detected using the qbda approach and in  terms of quantitation uh over 80 percent  of the mutations were within twofold of  the VF determined without enrichment  showing that although we are modifying  the vaf percentage of the sample we are  able to accurately quantitate the vaf in  in our library  and importantly uh because because we  are using this wild type suppressing  technology we were able to detect these  mutations using only four million  sequencing reads total  a significant reduction compared to deep  sequencing approaches  so next we applied the qbda AML panel to  a handful of retrospective clinical  samples collected from five ml patients  at time of diagnosis and also during  complete remission  uh so using qbda we were able to  identify a mutation in the npm1 gene at  .0052 vaf uh inpatient one and this was  missed by both flow cytometry and  conventional NGS this patient later  passed away due to relapse of the  disease  and in a second patient qbda as well as  other NGS did not observe npm1 mutations  in this case actually flow cytometry did  report mrd positivity NGS results at  later time points during relapse  confirmed the lack of npm1 mutations but  did detect de novo mutations in KDM 6A  and phf6 which we think suggests of this  patient experience clonal evolution  uh driving their driving their relapse  so while qbda did not detect these  mutations uh this appears to this this  likely is due to the makeup of the  initial prototype panel uh rather than  the technology itself our npm1 results  were consistently concordant with these  other NGS methods but we didn't have KDM  6A or phf6 on the panel but I think  you know the fact that we're in in at  least one patient we were able to  observe this npm1 mutation uh at .0052  vaf that was missed earlier and appeared  later on conventional NGS  um  suggests that this technology is very  promising for monitoring of residual  disease amplifications in acute myeloid  leukemia but I think the content itself  for the panel may need to be expanded to  function as a clinical uh mrd assay  and I do want to also be clear that all  of these samples here were used  retrospectively currently all of our  products are for research use only  so with that in mind we are actually a  project partner for an fnah biomarkers  Consortium project to establish new  methods for detecting disease in AML  uh we don't have anything specific to  share Beyond mentioning that we were  working on this at this time but we're  hoping to have some updates in the  coming months  now with that said uh in the in the  publication itself we do actually  demonstrate our ability to expand out  and broaden our panel coverage  so  in that paper we built a pan cancer  panel that covered 61 genes and over 6  300 mutations from the cosmic database  and this consisted of 360 applicants  split across two tubes and in the paper  we we test a variety of different uh  clinical sample types that were all  commercially obtained including cell  free DNA ffpe fresh frozen tumor tissue  um and also pbmc samples and and we show  that we're compatible with all of these  different types of samples but what I  wanted to highlight was really data that  was kind of similar to what I showed  earlier with the AML panel  um so using a number of contrived  samples that we prepared similar to what  we what I showed earlier we demonstrated  our ability to detect and quantify  mutations below point one percent vaf  and in some cases 0.01 the AF and showed  good concordance with a standard U NGS  method uh with with umis but without  enrichment  suggesting that we can really  significantly expand the content of our  panels while maintaining the performance  and the benefits of BDA  okay so just to highlight just to  summarize everything that I've gone  through today uh quantitative blocker  displacement amplification or qbda  enables highly multiplexed enrichment of  rare sequences such as smvs and indels  and detects and quantifies mutations  below 0.01 vaf using both tissue or  liquid samples  qbda reduces the relative fraction of  wild-type reads in NGS which enables  High sensitivity mutation sequencing  with reduced sequencing requirements  this potentially translates to better  cost efficiency as well as turnaround  time  uh qbda has been applied to clinical  longitudinal blood samples from AML  patients and we think that this has  great potential for longitudinal  monitoring than other liquid biopsy  applications  we were looking to further establish  collaborations that may be able to  further demonstrate some of these  benefits some potential benefits  okay so I wanted to next shift gears a  little bit and talk about new probe the  company  as as we are a startup and people may  not be fully aware of how how our  company works and how you can actually  work with new probe and access some of  these technologies that I've been  describing  so new probe is a developer of molecular  Technologies mainly focused in PCR and  NGS for improved detection of nucleic  acid biomarkers  so  new probe has its origins in Academia we  are a spin-out lab from using  Technologies from both Rice University  and the Weiss Institute at Harvard  with these Tech we've continued that  Spirit of technological innovation and  greatly expanded upon the original  technologies that our company started  with and developed a for a broader range  of Technologies designed to detect  different types of nucleic acid  biomarkers so the one that I talked  about today blocker displacement  amplification  uh is is designed for smbs and indels  but we're also working on  um you know other types of biomarkers  such as gene expression uh Gene fusions  as well as copy number variation  and so I wasn't planning I wasn't really  planning to go over these other  Technologies but just briefly in case  these are of Interest we have had some  other recent Publications also available  in nature Communications highlighting  some of these other Technologies uh  briefly of the first one quantitative  amplicon sequencing this is an  amplicon-based NGS workflow for Ultra  sensitive detection of copy number  variation down to five percent single  copy change in her2  as well as mutations down to 0.1 percent  vaf using low sample input  we also have expertise in primary design  we had we had we recently published  another paper about one of our primary  design algorithms for design of Highly  Multiplex PCR primer sets that minimize  primer dimer formation  uh this allows us to efficiently design  new Multiplex PCR and NGS assays  okay so we have this broad range of  Technologies but how do people work with  new probe  so what we mainly do is we leverage this  portfolio of Technologies and  collaborate with companies in the  biopharmaceutical Life Science and  diagnostic spaces and also work with  researchers who may who may be able to  utilize these Technologies  the main things that we do are sample  testing using defined NGS and PCR assays  and we also provide custom assay  development services to support  translational research programs we are  aware that you know research each  individual case is often different and  requirements May differ for different  assets  and so we we are prepared to rapidly  um  uh yeah rapidly  rapidly develop new solutions for  different biomarkers which you may need  just to briefly cover the current  offering for sample testing so we have  three assays that utilize the  quantitative blocker displacement  amplification technology that I  described today  and the first being  the AML mrd assay which I which I showed  data for earlier  and then also the pan cancer NGS assay  we also have an assay designed for  melanoma targets  on the qac side of things we have a her2  cnv assay and then we also have  Solutions using RNA for gene expression  as well as a number of different qpcr  assays  and all of these assays in a research  use only format are available  immediately for testing  I do want to describe our how our custom  assay development program works  so we follow a consultative model where  our team meets with your team and  discusses the specific problem that that  your team may be having with with  certain biomarker detection  and we come up with a solution that  utilizes our our Technologies our family  of Technologies  generally we can deliver a custom NGS  assay of about 200 amplicons in about 14  weeks and this is generally targeted at  translational research programs but  you know if if this is of Interest feel  free to reach out  okay so that brings me to the end of  this talk so  non-invasive liquid biopsies are an  important and emerging field in oncology  testing  new probe is developing a wide range of  Technologies which may have features  that improve liquid biopsies such as a  quantitative blocker displacement  amplification technology which I  described  and these Technologies are currently  available to biopharma life science and  research through our sample testing and  customized development services  all right with that I just want to thank  everyone for joining and we can take any  questions  great thank you so much Nick very  informative presentation we will now  start the live q a portion of the  webinar if you have a question you'd  like to ask please do so now just click  on the ask a question box located on the  far left of your screen and we'll answer  as many of your questions as we have  time for  now let's get started our first question  is how can you quantify the variant  allele frequency in the original sample  after BDA  yeah  so BDA can accurately quantify the  variant allele frequency of the original  sample uh but you're correct that BDA  also Alters the variant allele frequency  that we're measuring in the library  so this calculation is automatically  performed by our analysis software uh by  taking into account the input DNA amount  and you may Umi barcode and conversion  yield  and to accurately estimate input DNA we  also include internal positive controls  on the NGS panels that don't have  blockers that enable us to quantify  quantitate molecules at several  different loci in housekeeping genes  I also want to point out uh you know  since you know bioinformatics that can  sometimes be a bit of a black box I  think the most powerful piece of data  that we showed in this webinar is that  we highlighted some data from the  publication where we did actually  compare the the quantitation accuracy of  quantitative blocker displacement  amplification against  Umi NGS without enrichment and showed  good concordance between those two  methods showing that our method  um  in spite of altering the vaf  during enrichment we are able to  quantify the original vif with the  sample  thank you another question here has the  BDA technology been applied to other  biofluids specifically S A CSF  uh no not at this time that is something  that we would definitely be interested  in in trying uh because we think that  the same benefits that that we see in in  some in like blood or cell free DNA  should be applicable to salt and toes to  CSF as well but we just haven't been  able to actually  try that that would be an area of  interest for collaboration for us  yeah something we're very interested in  great thank you and the next question is  BDA available as a kit  uh yeah so currently we don't offer any  kitted products instead we offer ruo  sample testing services at our Houston r  d facility and that uses that menu of  predefined  um  predefined NGS assays and other qpcr  assays as well  yeah and you know like I also mentioned  another channel that BDA is accessible  or will be accessible uh will be through  biorad and their digital PCR assays  great thank you again Nick  free time today and your important  research  to thank lab roots and our sponsor new  probe for underwriting today's  educational webcast  before we go I like to thank the  audience for joining us today and  further questions questions we do not  have time for today and those submitted  during the on-demand period will be  addressed by the speaker via the contact  information you provided at the time of  registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  Roots will alert you via email when it's  available for replay and we encourage  you to share that email with any one of  your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time take care everyone  goodbye

